UPDATE care_chart_week_content SET move = $movejson${"format":"sections","reset":"360° Breathing, five rounds","move":"5-10 minute walk, whatever feels comfortable (Rebuild) / 10-15 minutes conversational activity (Thrive)","recoveryRoute":null,"build":["Wall Pelvic Tilts 6, Ankle Circles 6 per side (Restore)","Two rounds: Cat-Cow 8, Seated Pelvic Tilts 8 (Rebuild)","Two rounds: Cat-Cow 10, Supported Deep Squat Hold 15 sec, Seated Butterfly Stretch 1 min (Thrive)"],"release":"360° Breathing, slow and unhurried","door":null,"exercise":{"name":"Seated Butterfly Stretch (waiting-week framing)","focus":"continued hip openness while waiting","benefit":"Staying gently mobile now doesn't induce labour, but it does keep you comfortable while you wait.","mistake":"Trying to force labour to start through exercise — that isn't how this works.","tip":"Move for comfort, not to try to trigger anything."},"inRealLife":"This same stretch is a genuinely good way to pass restless waiting-room energy in these final days.","why":"Waiting is active work too — every quiet day is another day your baby is growing, and another day your body is preparing. There's no proven exercise or home method that reliably starts labour; movement in these final weeks is about your comfort, not about trying to induce anything.","quote":null,"note":"If your waters break, note the time, colour, and amount, and contact your care provider promptly, even without contractions yet.","safety":"Use stable support, breathe continuously, and keep effort conversational throughout. Continue avoiding extended flat-on-back positions — side-lying or propped-up versions work just as well. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness at rest or with fainting/palpitations, fluid leakage, one-sided leg swelling or calf pain, or any regular, rhythmic tightening that doesn't ease with rest — especially before 37 weeks.","recovery":null,"reflectionPrompt":null,"closingLabel":"A gentle fact","closingText":"nothing you do or don't do this week determines exactly when labour starts — that timing genuinely isn't in your control.","lookingAhead":"Full term technically begins next week — one more real milestone, whether or not your baby has arrived by then.","progressionNote":null,"clinicalFlag":null,"theme":"Waiting and watching","mantra":"Waiting is not the same as doing nothing.","milestone":null,"breathLegacy":null,"philosophy":null,"childLearned":null,"whatYouGaveYourself":null,"finalNote":null,"signatureLine":null,"tiers":null,"today":null}$movejson$::jsonb WHERE week_number = 38;
UPDATE care_chart_week_content SET move = $movejson${"format":"sections","reset":"360° Breathing, five rounds — take a moment to notice how far this same breath has come with you since week one","move":"5-10 minute walk, only if comfortable (Rebuild) / 10-15 minutes conversational activity (Thrive)","recoveryRoute":null,"build":["Choose your two favourite movements from the whole pregnancy — 5 minutes total (Restore)","One gentle round: Cat-Cow 8, Seated Pelvic Tilts 8, Supported Deep Squat Hold 15 sec (Rebuild)","One to two easy rounds, mixing in whichever movements from this pregnancy felt best (Thrive)"],"release":"360° Breathing, as long as feels good","door":null,"exercise":{"name":"360° Breathing (closing feature)","focus":"the one movement that's been with you since week one","benefit":"From ankle circles to squats to your own breath, this is the single practice that's stayed constant through every change your body has made.","mistake":"There's no wrong way to do this today.","tip":"Just breathe, wide and unhurried."},"inRealLife":"This exact breath will be one of the first tools you reach for in early labour, and again in the exhausted, overwhelmed moments of new motherhood after.","why":"Full term technically begins this week — but your body has already been doing exactly the right thing at exactly the right pace for 39 weeks, regardless of the calendar.","quote":null,"note":null,"safety":"Use stable support, breathe continuously, and keep effort conversational throughout. Continue avoiding extended flat-on-back positions — side-lying or propped-up versions work just as well. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness at rest or with fainting/palpitations, fluid leakage, one-sided leg swelling or calf pain, or any regular, rhythmic tightening that doesn't ease with rest — especially before 37 weeks.","recovery":null,"reflectionPrompt":"What is one thing about your body you've come to trust more over this pregnancy?","closingLabel":"Worth celebrating","closingText":"however and whenever your baby arrives, you have carried this entire pregnancy — every heavy week and every easy one — all the way here.","lookingAhead":"Whenever your baby arrives, movement continues — gently, differently, but it continues. The next chapter starts wherever you are, the same way this one did.","progressionNote":null,"clinicalFlag":null,"theme":"On the edge of full term","mantra":"However close this is to the end, you are still exactly where you need to be.","milestone":"If today becomes your baby's birthday... we'll meet you again on the other side.","breathLegacy":null,"philosophy":null,"childLearned":null,"whatYouGaveYourself":null,"finalNote":null,"signatureLine":null,"tiers":null,"today":null}$movejson$::jsonb WHERE week_number = 39;
UPDATE care_chart_week_content SET move = $movejson${"format":"sections","reset":"360° Breathing, lying or propped up, five slow rounds — this is genuinely enough for today if it's all there is","move":null,"recoveryRoute":{"vaginal or assisted":"A few slow steps around the room, whenever you feel ready — there's no schedule to meet","caesarean":"Even slower, shorter steps, supporting your incision with a pillow if it helps — up to your own pace entirely"},"build":["Gentle pelvic floor reconnection: a soft 3-second squeeze, 5 reps, whenever it feels comfortable — safe from day one, including after a caesarean","Ankle pumps, 10 per side, especially if you're spending a lot of time lying or sitting"],"release":"Rest. Genuinely, rest.","door":null,"exercise":{"name":"Pelvic Floor Reconnection","focus":"gently finding the connection again, not strength","benefit":"A soft, brief squeeze reintroduces the mind-muscle connection to a group of muscles that just did enormous work — this is about reconnecting, not building strength yet.","mistake":"Trying to do a long, hard squeeze right away.","tip":"Soft and brief is exactly right — 3 seconds is plenty this week."},"inRealLife":"This tiny, private practice is something you can do while feeding, holding your baby, or lying in bed — it needs no time set aside at all.","why":"Nothing about this week is about fitness — your body is actively healing from significant physical trauma, whether that trauma was a vaginal birth or surgery, and rest is the correct response to that.","quote":null,"note":"If walking feels surprisingly difficult after a caesarean, that isn't because you're weak — it's because you're recovering from major abdominal surgery while also becoming a parent. Avoid lifting anything heavier than your baby, and avoid any activity that strains your incision.","safety":"This is recovery, not training. Stop and call your care provider for: soaking through a pad in under an hour or passing large clots, a fever over 100.4°F/38°C, a severe headache with vision changes or upper-abdominal pain (possible postpartum preeclampsia — this can appear even with normal blood pressure during pregnancy, and up to 6 weeks after birth), calf pain or swelling on one side, redness/warmth/discharge at a C-section incision or tear, chest pain or breathlessness, or thoughts of harming yourself — these need same-day attention, not a wait-and-see approach.","recovery":["Bleeding soaking less than one pad per hour","Incision (if you have one) clean, without increasing pain or discharge","No fever over 100.4°F / 38°C","No severe headache or vision changes"],"reflectionPrompt":null,"closingLabel":"If today doesn't happen","closingText":"resting today, fully, is not a lesser version of this week — it is this week.","lookingAhead":"Bleeding (lochia) will keep changing over the next couple of weeks — bright red at first, gradually lighter — and that's expected, not a setback.","theme":"The first days","mantra":"You just did the hardest physical thing of your life. Today is about survival, not exercise.","childLearned":null,"whatYouGaveYourself":null,"progressionNote":null,"tiers":null,"signatureLine":null,"today":null,"milestone":null,"finalNote":null,"breathLegacy":null,"philosophy":null,"clinicalFlag":null}$movejson$::jsonb WHERE week_number = 40;
UPDATE care_chart_week_content SET move = $movejson${"format":"sections","reset":"360° Breathing, five rounds, wherever and however you're sitting or lying","move":null,"recoveryRoute":{"vaginal or assisted":"5 minute slow walk, if you feel up to it","caesarean":"3-5 minute very slow walk, supporting your incision if needed"},"build":["Pelvic floor reconnection: 3-second squeeze, 6 reps","Shoulder rolls, 10 — feeding positions are hard on the neck and shoulders"],"release":"A few slow breaths, eyes closed if you can manage it","door":null,"exercise":{"name":"Shoulder Rolls","focus":"releasing tension from feeding positions","benefit":"Hours of hunched feeding positions genuinely strain your neck and shoulders — this is real relief, not an afterthought.","mistake":"Rushing through them.","tip":"Slow and full circles, both directions."},"inRealLife":"If you're feeding, try these before or after a feed to release the neck tension long feeding sessions build up.","why":"Some days this week will feel harder than the last one — that's a normal, expected pattern of early recovery, not a sign that something's going backward.","quote":null,"note":null,"safety":"Same core guidance as week 0 — this is recovery, not training. Stop and seek care for heavy bleeding, fever, a severe headache with vision changes, calf pain/swelling, incision redness or discharge, chest pain/breathlessness, or thoughts of harming yourself.","recovery":["Bleeding gradually lightening, not suddenly heavier","Pain manageable most of the day (below 4/10)","No calf pain or swelling on one side","Getting sips of water and small meals in, even irregularly"],"reflectionPrompt":null,"closingLabel":"Notice this","closingText":"however uneven this week feels, your body is doing real, invisible healing work every single hour, moving or not.","lookingAhead":"Over the next week or two, your milk supply may shift and settle — this is common and usually not a sign of a problem.","theme":"Learning your new normal","mantra":"Recovery is not linear, and neither is this week.","childLearned":null,"whatYouGaveYourself":null,"progressionNote":null,"tiers":null,"signatureLine":null,"today":null,"milestone":null,"finalNote":null,"breathLegacy":null,"philosophy":null,"clinicalFlag":null}$movejson$::jsonb WHERE week_number = 41;
UPDATE care_chart_week_content SET move = $movejson${"format":"sections","reset":"360° Breathing, five rounds","move":null,"recoveryRoute":{"vaginal or assisted":"5-8 minute slow walk","caesarean":"5 minute slow walk, checking in with your incision as you go"},"build":["Pelvic floor reconnection: 4-second squeeze, 6 reps","Gentle pelvic tilts, lying or standing — 6 reps, whichever is more comfortable"],"release":"Rest, or a short lie-down if one is available","door":null,"exercise":{"name":"Gentle Pelvic Tilts","focus":"lower-back and pelvic awareness","benefit":"A very small, controlled movement that starts reconnecting you with your lower back and pelvis without any strain.","mistake":"Making the movement bigger than it needs to be.","tip":"Small and slow — barely visible from the outside is exactly right."},"inRealLife":"This tiny motion is close to the same gentle rock you'll find yourself doing instinctively to soothe your baby — and it's genuinely helpful after spending hours sitting to feed.","why":"Lochia (postpartum bleeding) typically moves through three stages over several weeks — bright red, then pinkish-brown, then a lighter cream or yellow — genuine healing happening in a predictable pattern, even though it can feel like it's taking a while.","quote":null,"note":"Bright red bleeding that returns after it had already lightened, or bleeding that's heavier than a normal period, is worth mentioning to your care provider.","safety":"Same core guidance as week 0 — this is recovery, not training. Stop and seek care for heavy bleeding, fever, a severe headache with vision changes, calf pain/swelling, incision redness or discharge, chest pain/breathlessness, or thoughts of harming yourself.","recovery":["Lochia colour moving toward pink or brown, not staying bright red","No return of bright red bleeding after it had already lightened","Low moments lifting within hours, not lasting all day","Incision (if you have one) continuing to look and feel better, not worse"],"reflectionPrompt":null,"closingLabel":"A gentle fact","closingText":"the changing color and amount of your bleeding is your body's own visible progress report — it doesn't need to match anyone else's timeline.","lookingAhead":"The next couple of weeks can bring a real, sometimes unexpected dip in mood for many mothers — it's worth knowing what's common before it happens.","theme":"Lochia and healing","mantra":"Your body is still actively healing beneath the surface.","childLearned":null,"whatYouGaveYourself":null,"progressionNote":null,"tiers":null,"signatureLine":null,"today":null,"milestone":null,"finalNote":null,"breathLegacy":null,"philosophy":null,"clinicalFlag":null}$movejson$::jsonb WHERE week_number = 42;
UPDATE care_chart_week_content SET move = $movejson${"format":"sections","reset":"360° Breathing, five rounds, hand resting on your belly","move":null,"recoveryRoute":{"vaginal or assisted":"8 minute slow walk","caesarean":"5-8 minute slow walk"},"build":["Pelvic floor reconnection: 4-second squeeze, 8 reps","Cat-Cow, very gentle range only, 6 reps, if it feels comfortable — skip entirely if it doesn't yet"],"release":"A few slow breaths, wherever you are","door":null,"exercise":{"name":"Cat-Cow (gentle reintroduction)","focus":"spinal mobility, reintroduced carefully","benefit":"A familiar movement from pregnancy, now reintroduced at the smallest possible range to start restoring general mobility.","mistake":"Pushing for the same range you had during pregnancy.","tip":"Tiny movement, well within comfort — there's no rush to get back to where you were."},"inRealLife":"Many mothers find this especially relieving after long feeding sessions hunched over a baby.","why":"Three weeks in is a genuinely common point for the initial adrenaline and support to fade while exhaustion is still very real — feeling harder now than in the first days doesn't mean anything has gone wrong.","quote":null,"note":"If you sometimes miss the person you were before becoming a mother, you're not doing motherhood wrong — you're adjusting to becoming someone new, and that takes time too.","safety":"Same core guidance as week 0 — this is recovery, not training. Stop and seek care for heavy bleeding, fever, a severe headache with vision changes, calf pain/swelling, incision redness or discharge, chest pain/breathlessness, or thoughts of harming yourself.","recovery":["Energy trending up even slightly, even if slowly","No new or worsening pain anywhere","Sadness or anxiety easing rather than building","Some support reachable if you need it today"],"reflectionPrompt":null,"closingLabel":"If today doesn't happen","closingText":"however hard this week feels, it is one week, not a permanent state.","lookingAhead":"Over the next week, it's worth knowing the real difference between the common 'baby blues' and something that needs more support.","theme":"The reality check","mantra":"Whatever this week feels like, it is not a preview of forever.","childLearned":null,"whatYouGaveYourself":null,"progressionNote":null,"tiers":null,"signatureLine":null,"today":null,"milestone":null,"finalNote":null,"breathLegacy":null,"philosophy":null,"clinicalFlag":null}$movejson$::jsonb WHERE week_number = 43;
UPDATE care_chart_week_content SET move = $movejson${"format":"sections","reset":"360° Breathing, five rounds","move":null,"recoveryRoute":{"vaginal or assisted":"8-10 minute walk","caesarean":"8 minute walk"},"build":["Pelvic floor reconnection: 5-second squeeze, 8 reps","Cat-Cow, 8 reps, if comfortable"],"release":"A few slow breaths","door":null,"exercise":{"name":"360° Breathing (check-in framing)","focus":"using breath as a genuine mood check-in","benefit":"A few slow, wide breaths can be a real moment to honestly notice how you're actually doing, not just how you're managing to appear.","mistake":"Treating this as one more task to get through quickly.","tip":"Let it be a genuine pause, even 30 seconds."},"inRealLife":"This same breath is available to you in the middle of a hard 3am feed, not just during a scheduled session.","why":"Baby blues — tearfulness, mood swings, feeling overwhelmed — are extremely common and usually ease within about two weeks of birth; feelings that are persisting, intensifying, or affecting your ability to function by now deserve a real conversation with your care provider, not just more time.","quote":null,"note":"If you're having any thoughts of harming yourself or your baby, that is genuinely urgent — contact your care provider or a crisis line right away, today.","safety":"This is recovery, not training. Stop and call your care provider for: soaking through a pad in under an hour or passing large clots, a fever over 100.4°F/38°C, a severe headache with vision changes or upper-abdominal pain (possible postpartum preeclampsia — this can appear even with normal blood pressure during pregnancy, and up to 6 weeks after birth), calf pain or swelling on one side, redness/warmth/discharge at a C-section incision or tear, chest pain or breathlessness, or thoughts of harming yourself — these need same-day attention, not a wait-and-see approach.","recovery":["Persistent sadness, anxiety, or intrusive thoughts lasting beyond two weeks","Bleeding mostly light or stopped by now","Any pain above 4/10 that isn't easing","A trusted person you could call today, if you needed to"],"reflectionPrompt":null,"closingLabel":"A gentle fact","closingText":"asking for help this week is not an overreaction — it is exactly the right response to a hard and real thing.","lookingAhead":"Over the next week or so, even small windows of movement can start feeling worthwhile again — no pressure if they don't yet.","theme":"Baby blues or something more","mantra":"Your feelings this week deserve real attention, not dismissal.","childLearned":null,"whatYouGaveYourself":null,"progressionNote":null,"tiers":null,"signatureLine":null,"today":null,"milestone":null,"finalNote":null,"breathLegacy":null,"philosophy":null,"clinicalFlag":null}$movejson$::jsonb WHERE week_number = 44;
UPDATE care_chart_week_content SET move = $movejson${"format":"sections","reset":"360° Breathing, five rounds","move":null,"recoveryRoute":{"vaginal or assisted":"10 minute walk, broken into shorter chunks if that's easier","caesarean":"8-10 minute walk"},"build":["Pelvic floor reconnection: 5-second squeeze, 10 reps","Cat-Cow, 8 reps","Gentle standing march in place, 10 per side, if that feels good"],"release":"A stretch of your choice, whatever feels good today","door":null,"exercise":{"name":"Standing March (gentle reintroduction)","focus":"reintroducing simple, low-impact leg movement","benefit":"A familiar, gentle movement that starts waking up your legs and hips again without any real strain.","mistake":"Turning this into a bigger workout than it needs to be.","tip":"Slow, small steps in place — this isn't about intensity yet."},"inRealLife":"This is close to the same instinctive sway many mothers do while holding a baby — you may already be doing a version of it without noticing.","why":"Even a few genuine minutes of movement, found wherever they fit, can start helping with mood and energy at this stage — it doesn't need to be a proper session to count. Sleep deprivation itself changes coordination, patience, appetite, and mood — if movement feels harder today than it \"should,\" poor sleep is often a big part of the real reason.","quote":null,"note":"If you had a caesarean, many mothers feel numbness, pulling, or tightness around the scar for months afterward — gentle scar desensitisation or massage, once your care provider confirms the incision has healed enough, can genuinely help.","safety":"Same core guidance as week 0 — this is recovery, not training. Stop and seek care for heavy bleeding, fever, a severe headache with vision changes, calf pain/swelling, incision redness or discharge, chest pain/breathlessness, or thoughts of harming yourself.","recovery":["Finding a few minutes of movement most days, however small","Pelvic heaviness staying steady or easing, not worsening","Getting even short stretches of real rest","Feeling genuinely supported, or knowing who to tell if not"],"reflectionPrompt":null,"closingLabel":"Worth celebrating","closingText":"however small today's window of movement was, finding it at all with a five-week-old is a real accomplishment.","lookingAhead":"Your six-week check-up is coming up soon — a good moment to bring any real questions, physical or emotional, rather than saving them for later.","theme":"Finding tiny windows","mantra":"A few minutes counts as real movement now.","childLearned":null,"whatYouGaveYourself":null,"progressionNote":null,"tiers":null,"signatureLine":null,"today":null,"milestone":null,"finalNote":null,"breathLegacy":null,"philosophy":null,"clinicalFlag":null}$movejson$::jsonb WHERE week_number = 45;
UPDATE care_chart_week_content SET move = $movejson${"format":"sections","reset":"360° Breathing, five rounds — take a moment to notice this breath has now carried you through pregnancy, birth, and six weeks of recovery","move":null,"recoveryRoute":{"vaginal or assisted":"10-12 minute walk","caesarean":"10 minute walk"},"build":["Pelvic floor reconnection: 5-second squeeze, 10 reps","Cat-Cow, 10 reps","Standing march, 10 per side"],"release":"A few slow breaths, and a moment of genuine rest","door":null,"exercise":{"name":"360° Breathing (six-week milestone)","focus":"the one practice that's carried through every stage so far","benefit":"The same breath from week one of pregnancy is still exactly what's needed here — recovery doesn't require a new tool, just this one, still.","mistake":"Feeling like you need something more advanced by now.","tip":"There's no wrong way to do this today — just breathe, wide and unhurried."},"inRealLife":"This breath will keep being useful long after this — in hard nights, hard days, and everyday moments still to come.","why":"The six-week mark is a genuine, meaningful checkpoint — but your care provider's clearance for more activity is about what's medically appropriate for your specific healing, not a universal deadline everyone reaches identically.","quote":null,"note":"Ask directly about any remaining pain, bleeding, mood concerns, or specific questions about returning to exercise — this appointment is built for exactly those conversations.","safety":"This is recovery, not training. Stop and call your care provider for: soaking through a pad in under an hour or passing large clots, a fever over 100.4°F/38°C, a severe headache with vision changes or upper-abdominal pain (possible postpartum preeclampsia — this can appear even with normal blood pressure during pregnancy, and up to 6 weeks after birth), calf pain or swelling on one side, redness/warmth/discharge at a C-section incision or tear, chest pain or breathlessness, or thoughts of harming yourself — these need same-day attention, not a wait-and-see approach.","recovery":["Real questions ready for your six-week visit","Any ongoing pain, bleeding, numbness, or mood concerns to raise","A clear sense of what's normal for your specific recovery, or knowing you can ask"],"reflectionPrompt":"What's one thing your body has done in the last six weeks that you didn't expect to be capable of?","closingLabel":"Worth celebrating","closingText":"six weeks of healing, feeding, and learning an entirely new rhythm is genuinely significant, whatever this particular week looked like.","lookingAhead":"Once you have your care provider's guidance, the next phase gently starts building real strength back — at your pace, on your own timeline, same as always.","theme":"The six-week marker","mantra":"A six-week check-up is a conversation, not a finish line.","childLearned":["Rested without apology, even when the to-do list didn't shrink.","Let herself be helped, again and again, without treating it as failure.","Trusted her own body's healing pace over anyone else's timeline.","Asked for support when something felt wrong, rather than staying quiet."],"whatYouGaveYourself":null,"progressionNote":null,"milestone":"Six weeks ago you were meeting your baby for the first time. Today you're already learning each other.","tiers":null,"signatureLine":null,"today":null,"finalNote":null,"breathLegacy":null,"philosophy":null,"clinicalFlag":null}$movejson$::jsonb WHERE week_number = 46;
UPDATE care_chart_week_content SET move = $movejson${"format":"sections","reset":"360° Breathing, five rounds","move":null,"recoveryRoute":{"vaginal or assisted":"10-12 minute walk","caesarean":"10 minute walk"},"build":["Pelvic floor: 5-second squeeze, 10 reps","Cat-Cow, 10 reps","Standing march, 10 per side"],"release":"A stretch of your choice","door":null,"exercise":{"name":"Wall Push-Ups","focus":"upper-body strength without any core strain","benefit":"Rebuilds arm and chest strength for the constant lifting and carrying ahead, with zero load on your healing core.","mistake":"Letting your hips sag out of a straight line.","tip":"Keep your body in one straight line, hands on a wall or a sturdy counter."},"inRealLife":"This is the exact strength you'll lean on lifting a car seat, a stroller, or your growing baby, again and again.","why":"If you didn't get full clearance at six weeks, that's not a setback — some healing, especially after a more complex delivery, genuinely takes longer, and your care team's pace is the right one to follow.","quote":null,"note":"If you had gestational diabetes, this is a good window to schedule your postpartum glucose screening, typically done 4-12 weeks after birth.","safety":"This phase still calls for patience, not intensity. Stop and seek care for pain above 4/10 that isn't easing, bleeding that returns after it had lightened, a fever, incision concerns, calf pain or swelling, persistent low mood or intrusive thoughts, or anything that feels wrong — none of that is something to push through.","recovery":["Any remaining pain from delivery easing, not worsening","Incision (if you have one) fully closed and comfortable","Energy beginning to feel a little more available","Clear on what your care provider actually cleared you for"],"reflectionPrompt":null,"closingLabel":"If today doesn't happen","closingText":"however clearance went, or didn't, your body is still healing exactly on its own timeline.","lookingAhead":"Over the next week or two, we'll start gently checking in with your core directly, the right way — not with crunches.","theme":"The six-week clearance, or not yet","mantra":"Clearance is about your body's timeline, not a fixed date.","childLearned":null,"whatYouGaveYourself":null,"progressionNote":null,"tiers":null,"signatureLine":null,"today":null,"milestone":null,"finalNote":null,"breathLegacy":null,"philosophy":null,"clinicalFlag":null}$movejson$::jsonb WHERE week_number = 47;
UPDATE care_chart_week_content SET move = $movejson${"format":"sections","reset":"360° Breathing, five rounds","move":null,"recoveryRoute":{"vaginal or assisted":"12-15 minute walk","caesarean":"10-12 minute walk"},"build":["A gentle core check: lying on your back, knees bent, lift your head slightly and notice any doming, coning, or bulging along your midline. You're simply noticing today, not trying to diagnose yourself — this tells you more than measuring a gap ever could","Pelvic floor: 5-second squeeze, 10 reps","Heel Slides, 8 per side"],"release":"A stretch of your choice","door":null,"exercise":{"name":"Heel Slides","focus":"safe, gentle core re-engagement","benefit":"Reintroduces real core control without any crunching or strain.","mistake":"Letting your lower back arch off the floor.","tip":"Keep your low back gently connected to the floor the whole time."},"inRealLife":"This same controlled core engagement is what protects your back the next time you bend down to lift your baby off the floor.","why":"Checking your midline now for doming, coning, or a sense of poor support — not just measuring a gap in finger-widths — is the more useful check; how your core functions day to day matters more than how it measures.","quote":null,"note":"If you notice ongoing doming, bulging, or a real sense your core isn't supporting you, that's worth mentioning to a pelvic health physiotherapist, not something to just push through.","safety":"Same core guidance as week 7 — patience over intensity. Stop and seek care for worsening pain, returning bleeding, fever, incision concerns, calf pain/swelling, persistent low mood, or anything that feels wrong.","recovery":["No doming, coning, or bulging noticed along your midline","No new pelvic heaviness or leaking with movement","Incision (if any) fully settled","Mood staying steady or improving"],"reflectionPrompt":null,"closingLabel":"A gentle fact","closingText":"your core's job right now is support, not appearance — that's the whole standard worth checking against.","lookingAhead":"Over the next week or two, you may notice yourself able to walk a little longer or a little faster without it costing as much.","theme":"Real core reconnection begins","mantra":"Checking in with your core is not the same as judging it.","childLearned":null,"whatYouGaveYourself":null,"progressionNote":null,"tiers":null,"signatureLine":null,"today":null,"milestone":null,"finalNote":null,"breathLegacy":null,"philosophy":null,"clinicalFlag":null}$movejson$::jsonb WHERE week_number = 48;
UPDATE care_chart_week_content SET move = $movejson${"format":"sections","reset":"360° Breathing, five rounds","move":null,"recoveryRoute":{"vaginal or assisted":"15-18 minute walk","caesarean":"12-15 minute walk"},"build":["Pelvic floor: 5-second squeeze, 10 reps","Heel Slides, 10 per side","Standing Row (light band), 10","Wall Push-Ups, 8"],"release":"A stretch of your choice","door":null,"exercise":{"name":"Standing Row (light band)","focus":"upper-back strength, revisited","benefit":"Rebuilds the posture-support strength that constant baby-carrying quietly wears down.","mistake":"Shrugging your shoulders up to your ears.","tip":"Squeeze your shoulder blades together, controlled pull."},"inRealLife":"This is the exact strength that keeps your posture from collapsing forward after months of carrying and feeding.","why":"More walking capacity is a genuine sign of healing, not an invitation to push further than feels good — the goal is still comfort and consistency, not distance.","quote":null,"note":null,"safety":"Same core guidance as week 7 — patience over intensity. Stop and seek care for worsening pain, returning bleeding, fever, incision concerns, calf pain/swelling, persistent low mood, or anything that feels wrong.","recovery":["Walks feeling more sustainable, not more draining","Core still feeling supportive during daily movement","Sleep still fragmented, but manageable most days","No new pain introduced by increased walking"],"reflectionPrompt":null,"closingLabel":"Worth celebrating","closingText":"walking longer without it draining you completely is real, measurable healing.","lookingAhead":"Over the next few weeks, some mothers notice their cycle beginning to return — we'll talk honestly about what that does and doesn't mean.","theme":"Longer walks, gentler pace","mantra":"More capacity doesn't mean you owe it more effort.","childLearned":null,"whatYouGaveYourself":null,"progressionNote":null,"tiers":null,"signatureLine":null,"today":null,"milestone":null,"finalNote":null,"breathLegacy":null,"philosophy":null,"clinicalFlag":null}$movejson$::jsonb WHERE week_number = 49;
UPDATE care_chart_week_content SET move = $movejson${"format":"sections","reset":"360° Breathing, five rounds","move":"15-18 minute walk, whichever pace feels right today","recoveryRoute":null,"build":["Pelvic floor: 5-second squeeze, 10 reps, consciously relaxing fully between reps too","Heel Slides, 10 per side","Standing Row, 10","Cat-Cow, 10"],"release":"A stretch of your choice","door":null,"exercise":{"name":"Pelvic Floor Coordination","focus":"pelvic floor as coordination, not just strength","benefit":"Matters for continence, core support, and comfort, regardless of whether your cycle has returned.","mistake":"Only squeezing, never practising the release too.","tip":"Consciously relax fully between reps, not just squeeze."},"inRealLife":"This same coordination is what protects you from leaking when you laugh, sneeze, or chase after your baby later.","why":"If you're exclusively and fully breastfeeding with no periods yet, the lactational amenorrhea method (LAM) can be a real temporary form of contraception — but it only works if you meet all of its specific criteria, so it's worth confirming exactly what those are with your care provider rather than assuming.","quote":null,"note":"If you have PCOS, your cycle's return can be less predictable — worth a specific conversation with your care provider about contraception if that's relevant for you, rather than guessing based on LAM alone. If you have a history of high blood pressure, some contraception options carry more clot risk than others, worth raising directly at this same conversation.","safety":"Same core guidance as week 7 — patience over intensity. Stop and seek care for worsening pain, returning bleeding, fever, incision concerns, calf pain/swelling, persistent low mood, or anything that feels wrong.","recovery":["No unusual or heavy bleeding outside a normal returning cycle","Comfortable asking your care provider about contraception if relevant","Core and pelvic floor still feeling supportive","Some real rest most days, even if imperfect"],"reflectionPrompt":null,"closingLabel":"A gentle fact","closingText":"cycle return, delayed or already back, both fall within a wide range of completely normal.","lookingAhead":"Over the next week, it's worth noticing how you're actually balancing rest against movement — neither one should be constantly losing.","theme":"Your cycle may be returning","mantra":"Whatever your body is doing, it isn't broken.","childLearned":null,"whatYouGaveYourself":null,"progressionNote":null,"tiers":null,"signatureLine":null,"today":null,"milestone":null,"finalNote":null,"breathLegacy":null,"philosophy":null,"clinicalFlag":null}$movejson$::jsonb WHERE week_number = 50;
UPDATE care_chart_week_content SET move = $movejson${"format":"sections","reset":"360° Breathing, five rounds","move":"15-20 minute walk, or split into smaller pieces through the day","recoveryRoute":null,"build":["Pelvic floor: 5-second squeeze, 10 reps","Heel Slides, 10 per side","Wall Push-Ups, 10","Standing Row, 10"],"release":"A stretch, or rest — whichever is truer today","door":null,"exercise":{"name":"Fitting movement into fragments","focus":"movement in an unpredictable day","benefit":"A few minutes between feeds or naps is genuinely enough to matter at this stage.","mistake":"Waiting for a perfect, uninterrupted block of time that may not come.","tip":"Break today's movement into two or three tiny pieces if one long session isn't realistic."},"inRealLife":"This is exactly how movement actually happens most days with a baby this age — in fragments, not one clean session.","why":"Rest and movement aren't actually competing for the same resource as much as they feel like they are — a few minutes of movement can genuinely help you feel more rested, not less.","quote":null,"note":null,"safety":"Same core guidance as week 7 — patience over intensity. Stop and seek care for worsening pain, returning bleeding, fever, incision concerns, calf pain/swelling, persistent low mood, or anything that feels wrong.","recovery":["Finding some movement most days, even in small pieces","Rest happening in some form, even imperfectly","Mood staying steady","No new or returning symptoms of concern"],"reflectionPrompt":null,"closingLabel":"Notice this","closingText":"however you split today between resting and moving, both were real forms of caring for yourself.","lookingAhead":"Next week marks three months since your baby arrived — worth a moment to notice everything that's changed since week zero.","theme":"When rest and movement compete","mantra":"You don't have to choose only one.","childLearned":null,"whatYouGaveYourself":null,"progressionNote":null,"tiers":null,"signatureLine":null,"today":null,"milestone":null,"finalNote":null,"breathLegacy":null,"philosophy":null,"clinicalFlag":null}$movejson$::jsonb WHERE week_number = 51;
UPDATE care_chart_week_content SET move = $movejson${"format":"sections","reset":"360° Breathing, five rounds — notice how this same breath has now carried you through birth and twelve full weeks of healing","move":"15-20 minute walk, or whatever feels right today","recoveryRoute":null,"build":["Choose your two favourite movements from this phase — 5 minutes total (lighter option)","One round: Heel Slides 10 per side, Wall Push-Ups 10, Standing Row 10, Cat-Cow 10 (fuller option)"],"release":"360° Breathing, as long as feels good","door":null,"exercise":{"name":"360° Breathing (closing feature)","focus":"the one practice that's stayed constant","benefit":"From ankle circles in early pregnancy to heel slides now, this breath has been the one thing that never changed.","mistake":"There's no wrong way to do this today.","tip":"Just breathe, wide and unhurried."},"inRealLife":"This breath is now genuinely part of you — reach for it in hard moments long after this programme ends.","why":"Three months is a real, meaningful marker — not because healing is finished, but because it's a good, honest moment to notice how far you've actually come since week zero.","quote":null,"note":"If you have a thyroid condition, this is a good time to confirm your postpartum labs are on schedule and continue your prescribed treatment — don't automatically attribute new fatigue only to early parenthood.","safety":"This phase still calls for patience, not intensity. Stop and seek care for pain above 4/10 that isn't easing, bleeding that returns after it had lightened, a fever, incision concerns, calf pain or swelling, persistent low mood or intrusive thoughts, or anything that feels wrong — none of that is something to push through.","recovery":["Feeling like yourself is returning, even if not fully yet","Physical healing continuing to trend in the right direction","Mood steady, or you know exactly who to tell if it isn't","Genuine questions for your next check-up, if you have one"],"reflectionPrompt":"What is one thing about mothering, or about your own body, that you understand now that you didn't twelve weeks ago?","closingLabel":"Worth celebrating","closingText":"twelve weeks of healing, learning, and adapting is genuinely one of the biggest things you will ever do.","lookingAhead":"From here, the next phase gently starts building real strength back — still at your pace, still on your own timeline, the same way it always has been.","theme":"Three months in","mantra":"Three months ago, you were just beginning to heal. Look at you now.","childLearned":["Checked in with her own body honestly, not just pushed through it.","Made room for both rest and movement, instead of choosing only one.","Asked real questions about her own health, without embarrassment.","Kept going gently, without rushing to be \"back to normal.\""],"whatYouGaveYourself":null,"progressionNote":null,"milestone":"Twelve weeks ago, you were meeting each other for the first time. Now look at how far you've both come.","philosophy":"There's still no catching up to do. There's only the next step, whenever you're ready.","tiers":null,"signatureLine":null,"today":null,"finalNote":null,"breathLegacy":null,"clinicalFlag":null}$movejson$::jsonb WHERE week_number = 52;
UPDATE care_chart_week_content SET move = $movejson${"format":"sections","reset":"360° Breathing, five rounds","move":"15-20 minute walk","recoveryRoute":null,"build":["Pelvic floor: 5-second squeeze, 10 reps","Heel Slides, 10 per side","Sit-to-Stand from a chair, 8 reps"],"release":"A stretch of your choice","door":null,"exercise":{"name":"Sit-to-Stand","focus":"functional leg strength for real daily tasks","benefit":"Mirrors the exact motion of standing up while holding your baby, over and over, every single day.","mistake":"Using your arms to push off instead of your legs.","tip":"Lead with your chest, drive through your heels."},"inRealLife":"This is the single movement you'll repeat more than almost any other exercise, all day, every day, for years.","why":"Rebuilding now means training for capability — the actual physical tasks of mothering — not chasing how your body looks.","quote":null,"note":null,"safety":"Build gradually — more weeks of consistency beats one hard session. Stop and seek care for pain above 4/10, new leaking or pelvic heaviness with exercise, doming or bulging along your midline, or anything that feels wrong. Persistent low mood, anxiety, or intrusive thoughts deserve real support at any point in this phase, not just early on.","recovery":["No pain above 4/10 during or after movement","No new leaking or heaviness with exercise","Energy trending in a workable direction","Mood steady, or support in place if not"],"reflectionPrompt":null,"closingLabel":"Worth celebrating","closingText":"thirteen weeks of healing behind you means this phase gets to be about building something new.","lookingAhead":"Over the next couple of weeks, if running is something you'd like to return to, we'll walk through a real readiness check first.","progressionNote":null,"theme":"Real rebuilding begins","mantra":"This phase is about capability, not appearance.","childLearned":null,"whatYouGaveYourself":null,"tiers":null,"signatureLine":null,"today":null,"milestone":null,"finalNote":null,"breathLegacy":null,"philosophy":null,"clinicalFlag":null}$movejson$::jsonb WHERE week_number = 53;
UPDATE care_chart_week_content SET move = $movejson${"format":"sections","reset":"360° Breathing, five rounds","move":"15-20 minute walk","recoveryRoute":null,"build":["A readiness check: 10 heel raises, 8 sit-to-stands, 8 step taps per side, and a 10-second single-leg balance per side — notice how each feels, not just whether you complete it","Pelvic floor: 5-second squeeze, 10 reps"],"release":"A stretch of your choice","door":null,"exercise":{"name":"Running-Readiness Self-Check","focus":"checking real readiness before adding impact","benefit":"Running is optional and can wait as long as you like — but if you want it back, this check is a genuinely useful first step before adding impact.","mistake":"Skipping straight to running without checking in first.","tip":"If any part of the check feels unstable or painful, that's useful information, not a failure — give it more time."},"inRealLife":"The same leg and core control this checks for is what keeps you steady chasing a toddler across a park later.","why":"This specific check — heel raises, sit-to-stands, step taps, single-leg balance — is a real, established way to gauge whether your body is ready for impact, rather than guessing based on how many weeks have passed.","quote":null,"note":"Running is entirely optional at any point in this journey — this check exists for mothers who want it back, not as something everyone needs to pass.","safety":"Same core guidance as week 13 — build gradually, more consistency over intensity. Stop for pain, new leaking or heaviness with exercise, midline doming/bulging, or anything that feels wrong.","recovery":["Readiness check completed without pain","No leaking or heaviness during the check","Balance felt reasonably steady","Feeling informed, not judged, by today's result"],"reflectionPrompt":null,"closingLabel":"A gentle fact","closingText":"however this check goes, it's information for today, not a verdict on your recovery.","lookingAhead":"If today's check felt steady, next week offers a gentle way to actually try some intervals — no pressure if you'd rather wait.","progressionNote":null,"theme":"Are you ready to run?","mantra":"Readiness is checked, not assumed.","childLearned":null,"whatYouGaveYourself":null,"tiers":null,"signatureLine":null,"today":null,"milestone":null,"finalNote":null,"breathLegacy":null,"philosophy":null,"clinicalFlag":null}$movejson$::jsonb WHERE week_number = 54;
UPDATE care_chart_week_content SET move = $movejson${"format":"sections","reset":"360° Breathing, five rounds","move":"8 minute walking warm-up, then (only if last week's check felt steady) four rounds of a 30-second gentle jog and a 90-second walk","recoveryRoute":null,"build":["Pelvic floor: 5-second squeeze, 10 reps","Sit-to-Stand, 10 reps"],"release":"A stretch of your choice","door":null,"exercise":{"name":"Walk-Run Intervals","focus":"a gentle, supported reintroduction to running","benefit":"Short jog intervals with long walk recoveries let your body test impact in small, manageable doses.","mistake":"Jumping straight to a longer continuous run.","tip":"Stop the jog intervals any day this doesn't feel good — walking is always the complete, valid version."},"inRealLife":"This same short-burst pacing is close to how you'll actually move once your baby is toddling — quick bursts, not steady marathons.","why":"Gradual walk-run intervals, not a full return to running all at once, is the safer, more established way back into impact for a postpartum body.","quote":null,"note":null,"safety":"Same core guidance as week 13 — build gradually, more consistency over intensity. Stop for pain, new leaking or heaviness with exercise, midline doming/bulging, or anything that feels wrong.","recovery":["No pain or heaviness during or after intervals","Recovering normally between jog segments","No new symptoms after trying impact","Feeling good about trying, regardless of the result"],"reflectionPrompt":null,"closingLabel":"If today doesn't happen","closingText":"sticking with walking today instead of intervals is a completely valid, correct choice, not a lesser one.","lookingAhead":"Over the next few weeks, we'll bring in more everyday capability — getting up off the floor, carrying, and lifting safely.","progressionNote":null,"theme":"First intervals, if you want them","mantra":"Trying this is optional, and so is loving it.","childLearned":null,"whatYouGaveYourself":null,"tiers":null,"signatureLine":null,"today":null,"milestone":null,"finalNote":null,"breathLegacy":null,"philosophy":null,"clinicalFlag":null}$movejson$::jsonb WHERE week_number = 55;
UPDATE care_chart_week_content SET move = $movejson${"format":"sections","reset":"360° Breathing, five rounds","move":"15-20 minute walk","recoveryRoute":null,"build":["Half-Kneeling to Standing transitions, 6 per side","Pelvic floor: 5-second squeeze, 10 reps","Sit-to-Stand, 10 reps"],"release":"A stretch of your choice","door":null,"exercise":{"name":"Half-Kneeling to Standing","focus":"safe, controlled floor-to-stand transitions","benefit":"A genuinely useful skill for getting up off the floor after playing with your baby, without straining your back or knees.","mistake":"Pushing straight up through your knees instead of stepping through.","tip":"Step one foot flat on the floor in front of you, then drive up through that leg."},"inRealLife":"You will do this exact movement more times a day than almost anything else once your baby is playing on the floor.","why":"Getting up off the floor smoothly is a real, trainable skill — worth practising deliberately rather than leaving to chance as your baby spends more time down there with you.","quote":null,"note":null,"safety":"Same core guidance as week 13 — build gradually, more consistency over intensity. Stop for pain, new leaking or heaviness with exercise, midline doming/bulging, or anything that feels wrong.","recovery":["Floor transitions feeling smoother, not more strained","No new knee, hip, or back discomfort","Core still feeling supportive","Energy holding steady through the day"],"reflectionPrompt":null,"closingLabel":"Notice this","closingText":"a smoother way up off the floor today is a small, real win, even if it doesn't feel like a big one.","lookingAhead":"Your baby's own mobility is likely to take off soon — crawling, then cruising, then walking — and this is exactly the capability that keeps you able to keep up. Next week, we'll focus on lifting safely — groceries, baby gear, and your growing baby included.","progressionNote":null,"theme":"Floor to standing, safely","mantra":"Getting up off the floor is a skill worth training on purpose.","childLearned":null,"whatYouGaveYourself":null,"tiers":null,"signatureLine":null,"today":null,"milestone":null,"finalNote":null,"breathLegacy":null,"philosophy":null,"clinicalFlag":null}$movejson$::jsonb WHERE week_number = 56;
UPDATE care_chart_week_content SET move = $movejson${"format":"sections","reset":"360° Breathing, five rounds","move":"15-20 minute walk, carrying something light if you'd like to practise","recoveryRoute":null,"build":["Hip Hinge Lift (practise with an empty laundry basket or a light box): bend at your hips and knees, keep the load close to your body, drive up through your legs, 8 reps","Suitcase Carry (one bag or weight in one hand, walk a short distance, switch sides), 2 trips per side","Pelvic floor: 5-second squeeze, 10 reps"],"release":"A stretch of your choice","door":null,"exercise":{"name":"Hip Hinge Lift","focus":"lifting mechanics for everyday objects — baby, laundry, car seat, groceries","benefit":"The single biggest factor in a safe lift isn't how much you're lifting, it's how — hips and knees bending, load close, no twisting.","mistake":"Rounding your back or twisting your spine while holding the load.","tip":"Bend at your hips and knees, keep whatever you're lifting close to your body, and drive up through your legs. Never twist through your spine while holding weight — turn your whole body by moving your feet instead."},"inRealLife":"This is the exact mechanics behind picking up your baby, a cot, a car seat, or a laundry basket safely, dozens of times a day, for years.","why":"Most postpartum back strain doesn't come from lifting too much — it comes from lifting the ordinary things of daily life (a baby, a basket, a car seat) with poor mechanics, over and over, until it adds up.","quote":null,"note":null,"safety":"Same core guidance as week 13 — build gradually, more consistency over intensity. Stop for pain, new leaking or heaviness with exercise, midline doming/bulging, or anything that feels wrong.","recovery":["Lifting felt controlled, not strained","No new back or shoulder discomfort","Core staying supportive under load","Overall energy trending steady or up"],"reflectionPrompt":null,"closingLabel":"Worth celebrating","closingText":"lifting something today with real, deliberate form is a skill that will protect your back for years, not just this week.","lookingAhead":"The next couple of weeks bring in more of the pushing and pulling strength that everyday life with a baby, and soon a mobile toddler, quietly demands.","progressionNote":null,"theme":"Lifting safely","mantra":"Mothers don't lift too little — they often just lift badly.","childLearned":null,"whatYouGaveYourself":null,"tiers":null,"signatureLine":null,"today":null,"milestone":null,"finalNote":null,"breathLegacy":null,"philosophy":null,"clinicalFlag":null}$movejson$::jsonb WHERE week_number = 57;
UPDATE care_chart_week_content SET move = $movejson${"format":"sections","reset":"360° Breathing, five rounds","move":"15-20 minute walk, pushing a stroller if you have one","recoveryRoute":null,"build":["Standing Row (light band), 10 reps","Wall Push-Ups, 10 reps","Sit-to-Stand, 10 reps"],"release":"A stretch of your choice","door":null,"exercise":{"name":"Standing Row and Wall Push-Ups, paired","focus":"the two directions of everyday strength — pulling and pushing","benefit":"Between them, these two movements cover almost every pushing and pulling task in a day with a baby.","mistake":"Rushing through either one for speed instead of control.","tip":"Slow and controlled in both directions works both muscles harder than fast and loose."},"inRealLife":"This exact pair of strengths is what makes pushing a stroller uphill, or pulling a car door open with a baby on your hip, feel manageable instead of exhausting.","why":"Almost everything physically demanding about caring for a baby is either a push or a pull — training both directions on purpose builds real, usable strength for exactly that, and it's exactly the strength a soon-to-be-mobile toddler will keep demanding more of.","quote":null,"note":"It's worth knowing the difference between expected discomfort and a real warning sign. Expected: ordinary muscle soreness a day or two after new effort, mild scar pulling or tightness that eases with movement, or a feeling of general fatigue. Worth mentioning to your care provider: pain that's sharp or above 4/10, pelvic heaviness that's new or worsening, joint pain that doesn't ease with rest, or any leaking with exercise. When in doubt, it's always reasonable to ask.","safety":"Same core guidance as week 13 — build gradually, more consistency over intensity. Stop for pain, new leaking or heaviness with exercise, midline doming/bulging, or anything that feels wrong.","recovery":["Pushing and pulling movements feeling controlled, not strained","Posture holding up through the day","No new joint discomfort","Energy adequate for daily tasks"],"reflectionPrompt":null,"closingLabel":"A gentle fact","closingText":"the strength you're building here shows up in ordinary moments you won't even notice as \"exercise\" once it's working.","lookingAhead":"Around four months postpartum, some mothers notice a new kind of tiredness worth knowing about — we'll talk about what that can be next week.","progressionNote":"If Standing Row feels easy by now, a slightly stronger resistance band is a reasonable next step — no need to rush it.","theme":"Pushing, pulling, and everyday strength","mantra":"Strollers, groceries, and car seats are your real gym equipment now.","childLearned":null,"whatYouGaveYourself":null,"tiers":null,"signatureLine":null,"today":null,"milestone":null,"finalNote":null,"breathLegacy":null,"philosophy":null,"clinicalFlag":null}$movejson$::jsonb WHERE week_number = 58;
UPDATE care_chart_week_content SET move = $movejson${"format":"sections","reset":"360° Breathing, five rounds","move":"15-20 minute walk","recoveryRoute":null,"build":["Sit-to-Stand, 10 reps","Standing Row, 10 reps","Half-Kneeling to Standing, 6 per side"],"release":"A stretch of your choice","door":null,"exercise":{"name":"Sit-to-Stand (revisited)","focus":"steady functional strength, whatever your energy is doing","benefit":"This movement stays useful and appropriate whether you're feeling strong or unusually drained this week.","mistake":"Pushing through unusual fatigue instead of paying attention to it.","tip":"If today is harder than it should be, that itself is worth noting, not just pushing past."},"inRealLife":"However your energy is this week, this is still the movement you'll lean on most, lifting your baby again and again.","why":"Postpartum thyroiditis can appear anywhere from about one to eight months after birth — often in two phases, a period of feeling wired and anxious followed later by a period of heavy, unusual fatigue — and it can happen even without any prior thyroid history, so it's worth knowing about rather than automatically blaming every hard week on early parenthood.","quote":null,"note":"Unusual fatigue that doesn't match your sleep, a racing heart, unexplained weight change, or mood swings that feel different from ordinary tiredness are worth raising with your care provider — a simple blood test can check this.","safety":"Build gradually — more weeks of consistency beats one hard session. Stop and seek care for pain above 4/10, new leaking or pelvic heaviness with exercise, doming or bulging along your midline, or anything that feels wrong. Persistent low mood, anxiety, or intrusive thoughts deserve real support at any point in this phase, not just early on.","recovery":["Fatigue matching your actual sleep, not exceeding it","No unexplained racing heart or unusual weight change","Mood swings feeling proportionate, not extreme","Comfortable raising this at your next appointment if something feels off"],"reflectionPrompt":null,"closingLabel":"A gentle fact","closingText":"asking your care provider about this is simply being thorough, not overreacting to normal new-parent tiredness.","lookingAhead":"Over the next few weeks, we'll keep building real stamina and strength, at whatever pace your body is actually offering.","progressionNote":null,"theme":"When fatigue isn't just tiredness","mantra":"Not all exhaustion is explained by a new baby.","childLearned":null,"whatYouGaveYourself":null,"tiers":null,"signatureLine":null,"today":null,"milestone":null,"finalNote":null,"breathLegacy":null,"philosophy":null,"clinicalFlag":null}$movejson$::jsonb WHERE week_number = 59;
UPDATE care_chart_week_content SET move = $movejson${"format":"sections","reset":"360° Breathing, five rounds","move":"20 minutes of walking, swimming, or cycling","recoveryRoute":null,"build":["Sit-to-Stand, 12 reps","Standing Row, 10 reps","Wall Push-Ups, 10 reps","Suitcase Carry, 2 trips per side"],"release":"A stretch of your choice","door":null,"exercise":{"name":"20-minute conditioning session","focus":"building general stamina, not a single skill","benefit":"Simply doing 20 sustained minutes regularly is what actually builds the stamina an active toddler-to-be will demand.","mistake":"Judging today by speed or distance instead of consistency.","tip":"The same 20 minutes, done most weeks, matters more than one exceptional session."},"inRealLife":"This is the stamina behind an entire afternoon at the park without needing to sit down every few minutes.","why":"Stamina builds through consistent, unremarkable repetition far more than through occasional hard efforts — the boring, steady version of this actually works better.","quote":null,"note":null,"safety":"Same core guidance as week 13 — build gradually, more consistency over intensity. Stop for pain, new leaking or heaviness with exercise, midline doming/bulging, or anything that feels wrong.","recovery":["Sustained activity feeling manageable, not depleting","Recovery between sessions feeling adequate","No new pain with increased duration","Motivation holding up, or okay if it isn't every week"],"reflectionPrompt":null,"closingLabel":"Worth celebrating","closingText":"an ordinary, consistent week like this one is exactly what real stamina is built from.","lookingAhead":"Over the next few weeks, expect more floor-based capability — since that's where a lot of your time with your baby is likely heading.","progressionNote":"If this feels comfortable and your baby is happy to be held for a few reps, holding them during Sit-to-Stand is a natural next step. If they wiggle or protest, that's a perfectly good reason to skip it today — there's no version of this that requires a cooperative baby.","theme":"Rebuilding your stamina","mantra":"Stamina is built in ordinary weeks, not dramatic ones.","childLearned":null,"whatYouGaveYourself":null,"tiers":null,"signatureLine":null,"today":null,"milestone":null,"finalNote":null,"breathLegacy":null,"philosophy":null,"clinicalFlag":null}$movejson$::jsonb WHERE week_number = 60;
UPDATE care_chart_week_content SET move = $movejson${"format":"sections","reset":"360° Breathing, five rounds","move":"15-20 minute walk","recoveryRoute":null,"build":["Half-Kneeling to Standing, 8 per side","Sit-to-Stand, 10 reps","A few minutes of floor play, deliberately moving between sitting, kneeling, and standing"],"release":"A stretch of your choice","door":null,"exercise":{"name":"Floor-play mobility circuit","focus":"moving comfortably between floor positions","benefit":"Real, sustained floor play means moving between sitting, kneeling, and standing dozens of times — training this directly makes it less tiring.","mistake":"Staying in one position the whole time to avoid the transitions.","tip":"Practise moving between positions on purpose today, not just holding one."},"inRealLife":"This is quite literally what an afternoon of floor play with your baby demands — not strength in one position, but ease moving between all of them.","why":"A huge amount of early parenting happens on the floor — training the transitions themselves, not just isolated strength, is what actually makes that sustainable.","quote":null,"note":null,"safety":"Same core guidance as week 13 — build gradually, more consistency over intensity. Stop for pain, new leaking or heaviness with exercise, midline doming/bulging, or anything that feels wrong.","recovery":["Floor transitions feeling easier than a few weeks ago","No new knee or back discomfort","Energy adequate for sustained play","Enjoying movement, at least some of the time"],"reflectionPrompt":null,"closingLabel":"Notice this","closingText":"however long you lasted on the floor today, that's real, physical work most people never think to call exercise.","lookingAhead":"If you've been missing a sport or activity you used to love, the next week is a good time to think honestly about easing back into it.","progressionNote":null,"theme":"Play on the floor","mantra":"Getting down to your baby's level is its own kind of strength.","childLearned":null,"whatYouGaveYourself":null,"tiers":null,"signatureLine":null,"today":null,"milestone":null,"finalNote":null,"breathLegacy":null,"philosophy":null,"clinicalFlag":null}$movejson$::jsonb WHERE week_number = 61;
UPDATE care_chart_week_content SET move = $movejson${"format":"sections","reset":"360° Breathing, five rounds","move":"20 minutes of your choice — walking, swimming, cycling, or your first easy try at something you used to do (yoga, badminton, dancing, the gym, hiking — whatever it is for you)","recoveryRoute":null,"build":["Sit-to-Stand, 10 reps","Standing Row, 10 reps","Suitcase Carry, 2 trips per side"],"release":"A stretch of your choice","door":null,"exercise":{"name":"A gentler first return","focus":"easing back into an activity you used to love","benefit":"Returning at maybe 50-60% of your old effort or duration lets you test how your body actually responds before building back up.","mistake":"Trying to pick up exactly where you left off before pregnancy.","tip":"Treat your first session back as information-gathering, not a real workout yet."},"inRealLife":"This same patient, gradual approach is what actually gets you back to the activity you love, rather than a setback from doing too much too soon.","why":"A body that's grown and delivered a baby genuinely isn't the same body that last did that activity — whether it's yoga, badminton, dancing, the gym, hiking, or anything else, a real conversation with your care provider or a pelvic health physiotherapist first is worth having for anything high-impact or intense, not just diving back in.","quote":null,"note":null,"safety":"Same core guidance as week 13 — build gradually, more consistency over intensity. Stop for pain, new leaking or heaviness with exercise, midline doming/bulging, or anything that feels wrong.","recovery":["First return attempt felt manageable, not overwhelming","No new pain or heaviness afterward","Realistic expectations feeling okay, not discouraging","Clear on when to check with a professional before progressing further"],"reflectionPrompt":null,"closingLabel":"A gentle fact","closingText":"wanting the old version of an activity back is completely normal — arriving there gradually is still arriving.","lookingAhead":"Over the next couple of weeks, we'll bring back a bit more balance and coordination work as your overall capability keeps building.","progressionNote":null,"theme":"Returning to what you loved","mantra":"Wanting your old activity back doesn't mean starting exactly where you left off.","childLearned":null,"whatYouGaveYourself":null,"tiers":null,"signatureLine":null,"today":null,"milestone":null,"finalNote":null,"breathLegacy":null,"philosophy":null,"clinicalFlag":null}$movejson$::jsonb WHERE week_number = 62;
UPDATE care_chart_week_content SET move = $movejson${"format":"sections","reset":"360° Breathing, five rounds","move":"15-20 minute walk","recoveryRoute":null,"build":["Single-leg balance, 15-20 seconds per side, near support","Sit-to-Stand, 10 reps","Half-Kneeling to Standing, 8 per side"],"release":"A stretch of your choice","door":null,"exercise":{"name":"Single-Leg Balance","focus":"coordination and stability, revisited from pregnancy","benefit":"The same balance work from pregnancy, now rebuilding after a body that's changed shape twice in one year.","mistake":"Looking down at your feet instead of ahead.","tip":"Keep a wall or chair within reach, and pick a point ahead to focus on."},"inRealLife":"This is the exact stability behind bending down to catch a toddler mid-stumble without losing your own footing.","why":"Your balance genuinely shifted during pregnancy and shifted again after birth — rebuilding it deliberately now, rather than assuming it will just return, tends to work better.","quote":null,"note":null,"safety":"Same core guidance as week 13 — build gradually, more consistency over intensity. Stop for pain, new leaking or heaviness with exercise, midline doming/bulging, or anything that feels wrong.","recovery":["Balance feeling steadier than a few weeks ago","No dizziness or unusual unsteadiness","Core still feeling supportive during single-leg work","Confidence in movement generally improving"],"reflectionPrompt":null,"closingLabel":"Worth celebrating","closingText":"however steady you felt today, it's more than you had a few months ago, and that's worth noticing.","lookingAhead":"Over the next couple of weeks, it's worth taking a moment to notice how differently you see your body now compared to before this whole journey.","progressionNote":null,"theme":"Balance and coordination, revisited","mantra":"Your balance has changed at least twice this year — it's allowed to keep changing.","childLearned":null,"whatYouGaveYourself":null,"tiers":null,"signatureLine":null,"today":null,"milestone":null,"finalNote":null,"breathLegacy":null,"philosophy":null,"clinicalFlag":null}$movejson$::jsonb WHERE week_number = 63;
UPDATE care_chart_week_content SET move = $movejson${"format":"sections","reset":"360° Breathing, five rounds","move":"20 minutes of your choice","recoveryRoute":null,"build":["Sit-to-Stand, 12 reps","Standing Row, 10 reps","Suitcase Carry, 2 trips per side"],"release":"A stretch of your choice","door":null,"exercise":{"name":"Whichever movement feels strongest to you today","focus":"trusting your own judgment about your own body","benefit":"By this point, you know your own body's signals better than any generic plan does — following that instinct is the actual skill.","mistake":"Second-guessing what your body is telling you in favour of what a plan says.","tip":"If something feels genuinely ready for more, it probably is — trust that as much as the plan."},"inRealLife":"This same trust in your own read of your body is exactly what you'll lean on for every parenting decision still ahead, physical or otherwise.","why":"A body that's grown, delivered, and fed a baby is genuinely different from the one before — different, not lesser, and increasingly worth trusting on its own terms rather than measuring against an old standard.","quote":"If you sometimes still miss the body you had before, that feeling can coexist with real pride in what this one has done — neither one cancels the other out.","note":null,"safety":"Same core guidance as week 13 — build gradually, more consistency over intensity. Stop for pain, new leaking or heaviness with exercise, midline doming/bulging, or anything that feels wrong.","recovery":["Feeling more at ease in your body than a month ago","Energy and strength both trending in a good direction","Mood steady, or supported if it isn't","A sense of trust in your own read of your body"],"reflectionPrompt":null,"closingLabel":"Notice this","closingText":"trusting your body a little more this week, even briefly, is real progress worth noticing.","lookingAhead":"Half a year since birth arrives in the next couple of weeks — worth pausing on when it does.","progressionNote":"If Wall Push-Ups feel easy now, an Incline Push-Up on a sturdy countertop or bench is a reasonable next step, well before ever needing to work toward the floor.","theme":"Trusting your changed body","mantra":"Different is not the same as worse.","childLearned":null,"whatYouGaveYourself":null,"tiers":null,"signatureLine":null,"today":null,"milestone":null,"finalNote":null,"breathLegacy":null,"philosophy":null,"clinicalFlag":null}$movejson$::jsonb WHERE week_number = 64;
